Dear all,

thanks to the help provided in this group, my model is now finally
converging properly.
In the end I re-parametrized 6 of the 7 group-level parameters with the
non-central form and roughly scaled them into similar ranges. For one
parameter this resulted in problems, so I left this one with in the
original version. Furthermore, I added some slightly(?) informative
priors for the group level parameters.

Here are diagnostics plots of ALL the parameters on all levels.

http://homepages.uni-paderborn.de/jeti/diag/

I think everything looks fine, doesn't it? Somewhere there is a bi-modal
posterior on the subject level, but I don't think this is any problem in
principle.

One thing remains where I am a bit unsure. I find it difficult to judge
how informative the priors are. I would like to keep it pretty
conservative, so I tried to only avoid extreme values. Furthermore, I
have an experimental and one neutral control condition. I used priors
(at least for the means) that reflect the neutral state (e.g., no shift
effect, equally distributed weights, etc.) and are quite diffuse for
both conditions.

I plotted the posteriors for parameters from each condition together
with a visualization of their common prior:

http://homepages.uni-paderborn.de/jeti/priors/

I'm very interested in your opinions regarding the informativeness of
these priors? From what I see the data clearly dominate the distributions.

And do you think using the "neutral" prior as described above for both
conditions is a conservative choice? As I'm interested in the
differences neutral-experimental, it could only reduce the detected effects.

Many thanks & best regards
Jan

