Hello!

Thanks for accepting me into the group and also creating a software like stan!
I am collaborating with a mathematician to fit some complicated biological process and got stuck in the middle of the design.
I read the manual, but couldn't find the answer I wanted, so here I ask. My questions are three fold.

1. I am trying to construct a model involving a distribution characterized as a sum of N-many iid Pareto random variables where N itself is a Poisson distributed. Is such thing possible in stan?

//pseudo code
N ~ poisson(lambda)
for (i in 1:N) { //
    M += pareto(y_min, alpha)
}

That is, can I use for loop based on a parameter? ( I saw Michael's lecture and he sort of mentions something alone the line (http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xWQpEAyI5s8&t=16m34s) but wanted to ask in the context of my problem). If so how?

2. I learned that there is a approximation for this sort of compound Poisson when lambda is >=100, which is Landau distribution

X ~ Landau  // pdf has the form : 1/pi * integral from 0 to infinity (e ^-(-t*log(t)-xt) * sin(pi * t)) dt)
M = y_min * lambda (X + ln(lambda))

But then again, can Landau distribution be implemented in stan when it involves integral?

3. If #1 and #2 do not work, can I then pass the lookup table of log Landau pdf (generated from mathematica) to stan since its parameter-less?  As far as I know, the only interface to giving a custom distribution to stan is via increment_log_prob, but I was curious if "the passing the lookup table"  is possible.

Any help/advice is appreciated! thank you very much!!

Best,
Albert

From ua5e83218c834e013 at groups.google.com  Wed Nov 11 08:40:51 2015
From: ua5e83218c834e013 at groups.google.com (Michael Betancourt)
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2015 08:40:51 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] Information heterogeneity across hierarchies
Message-ID: <k9PPoZvrAUM.dm8fB8ZCBQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <k9PPoZvrAUM.C2VsAF4rBQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <k9PPoZvrAUM.RTtVSrsjBQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com> <k9PPoZvrAUM.C2VsAF4rBQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/k9PPoZvrAUM/m/dm8fB8ZCBQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Yes, one of the awkward features of deep hierarchical models (with many layers,
we usually reserve levels to speak about different effects on the same layer)
is that variance grows exponentially fast so you have to have tighter priors than
you might initially consider.  For example, try sampling from the hierarchical prior
and see how much variance you get.  This is also a nice way to tune the priors
to ensure reasonable variance of the top-level parameters.

As Krzysztof notes you’ll also want to be careful about the implementation.
There are 2^{nodes} possible parameterizations but a fully non-centered
one will probably be a good starting point.  You’ll ultimately get something
that looks like

top_level_effect = mean_effect + \prod_{n = 1}^{N_layers} standard_deviation_{n}

