Ok so I should aim to have unconstrained parameters on the unit scale where possible. With this parameter f this is no issue. However there are parameters in my full model which I would like to constrain to be positive, to avoid singularities. I am using the linear model and data above to try to learn a bit more about stan and so have tried constraining this model to learn about the behaviour this leads to.

Firstly I've tried imposing a constraint that f must be greater than 0 and using an exponential prior. The model code for this is,

  data {
    int<lower=0> N;
    vector[N] V;
    vector[N] P;
    real<lower=0> sigma;
  }
  parameters {

    real<lower=0> f;
  }
  model{
    vector[N] mu;
    f ~ exponential(1);
    for (n in 1:N) mu[n] <- 1 + f*(1/(10*V[n]-0.5))^6;
    P ~ normal(mu, sigma);
  }
After an initial run I've increased adapt_delta and max_treedepth as prompted by the reported messages from stan and the attached exponentialprior_increasedadaptdelta.png is the resulting traceplot. There is 1 chain near 0 which is drifting slowly upwards whilst the others all converge. Is this 1 chain performing poorly due to being near the hard constraint. Furthermore I sometimes receive messages of the following type,

The following numerical problems occured the indicated number of times on chain 4
                                                                                                                               count
Exception thrown at line 15: normal_log: Location parameter[1] is inf, but must be finite!    18
If this warning occurs sporadically, such as for highly constrained variable types like covariance matrices, then the sampler is fine,but if this warning occurs often then your model may be either severely ill-conditioned or misspecified.

The other thing I've tried is parameterising in terms of f* where f=(f*)^2, so that f must be positive whilst f* is unconstrained. The model for this is as follows,
simple_EoS_code<-'

  data {
    int<lower=0> N;
    vector[N] V;
    vector[N] P;
    real<lower=0> sigma;
  }
  parameters {

    real f_star;
  }
  model{
    vector[N] mu;
    f_star ~ normal(0,1);
    for (n in 1:N) mu[n] <- 1 + (f_star^2)*(1/(10*V[n]-0.5))^6;
    P ~ normal(mu, sigma);
  }
Now again increasing adapt_delta and max_treedepth when prompted I find once more that 1 out of 50 chains, which starts close to 0, is drifting slowly rather than converging. squaredparameters_increaseadaptdelta.png is the plots the traces for this model.Is this an issue with the multimodality of the posterior or something else.

Any feedback would be welcome.

Thanks,
Michael

