> Hi all,

Thanks for the tips. I've decided to abandon the complicated part for the moment and just make sure the measurement error part is working, so I simplified things a little.

I'm simulating data from a power-law and then fitting the log10 in Stan. I do the simulation and transformation in Python and feed it to Pystan. My main concern is that when I included noise in X and then try to recover the slope, the slope is biased a bit. the bias is the same regardless of if I include measurement error leading me to believe that the model is not working properly. Here is the function that generates the simulated data. For now I'm just using it to create one data set:

###### PYTHON CODE

def SimulateData(numSets=6,pointsLo=4,pointsHi=20,alpha_cent=1.E53,beta_cent=1.38,varyA=False,varyB=True):
    xs   = []
    ys   = []
    idxs = []
    sigXs= []
    sigYs= []
    alphas= []
    betas = []

    random.seed(15)
    for i in range(numSets):

        numData = random.random_integers(pointsLo,pointsHi)
        print numData
        xi = 10**random.uniform(1.5,3,size=numData)
        alpha=alpha_cent
        if varyA:
            alpha = random.normal(loc=alpha_cent, scale=4.E52)
        else:
            alpha = alpha_cent
        if varyB:
            beta = random.normal(loc=beta_cent, scale=.1)
        else:
            beta = beta_cent

        alphas.append(alpha)
        betas.append(beta)

        # Rest frame
        eta = alpha*((xi/100.)**beta)# + epsilon

        #FE

        zeta = eta
        zxi=xi

        ysig = zeta*(.2+random.rand(len(zeta))/10. )
        xsig = zxi*(.2+random.rand(len(zeta))/10. )

        x = random.normal(loc=zxi, scale=xsig)
        y = random.normal(loc=zeta, scale=ysig)

        xs.extend(x)
        ys.extend(y)
        sigXs.extend(xsig)
        sigYs.extend(ysig)
        idxs.extend([i+1]*numData)

    xs = array(xs)
    ys = array(ys)
    idxs=array(idxs)
    sigXs=array(sigXs)
    sigYs=array(sigYs)

    return xs, sigXs, ys, sigYs, idxs, alphas, betas

#####

Then I create the data dictionary like so:

data= {"N":len(xs),"x_meas":log10(xs),"y":log10(ys), "xsig":sigXs/(xs * log(10.)),"ysig":sigYs/(ys * log(10.)),"maxSlope":2.5}

And the Stan code for this model is:

 """
data {

    int<lower=0> N;         // Number of points

    vector[N] y;             //FE

    vector[N] x_meas;        // measurement of x

    vector[N] ysig;
    vector[N] xsig;

    real maxSlope;

}

parameters {

    vector[N] x;              //unknown true value

    real B0;               // intercept
    real beta;           //slope

    vector<lower=0>[N] nuMinusOne_x;
     vector<lower=0>[N] nuMinusOne_y;

}

transformed parameters{

    vector<lower=0>[N] nu_x;
    vector<lower=0>[N] nu_y;

    nu_x <- nuMinusOne_x + 1;
    nu_y <- nuMinusOne_y + 1;

}

model {

    // Set meausurement error priors

    x_meas ~ student_t( nu_x, x, xsig); // measurement model

    y ~ student_t( nu_y, B0 + beta *( x -2.), ysig);

    beta  ~ normal(0.,maxSlope);

    B0  ~ normal(53.,1.);

    nuMinusOne_x ~exponential(1./29.);
    nuMinusOne_y ~exponential(1./29.);

} """

As you can see I'm using student-t, though I simulate normal errors. I don't include a hyper-prior on the x's because the errors are heteroscedastic. From what I can tell, this should work. If I simply don't include noise on the measured x's in the simulations and remove that part of the model, then the slopes come out very well. I'm not sure what type of prior ot put on the x's, I've tried a normal prior but the results become wonky. What am I missing?

Cheers!

