From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Thu Nov 19 22:38:27 2015
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2015 22:38:27 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] Hierarchical heteroscedastic measurement error--- unstable?
Message-ID: <p7ggo18jIFQ.Rhe3lh_lBwAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <p7ggo18jIFQ.HAupwy_PBwAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <p7ggo18jIFQ.NiNSZQuFAQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com> <p7ggo18jIFQ.HAupwy_PBwAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/p7ggo18jIFQ/m/Rhe3lh_lBwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I'm not sure if this is your problem, but you have a potentially
very fat-tailed prior on x and only a single observation x_meas to fit
each parameter x with.

You can also have problems with parameters like B0 that you expect
to be around 53.  Stan has to figure that out during adaptation.  We
often recommend the non-centered style parameterization of this, where you'd
have

  B0_raw ~ normal(0, 1);
  B0 <- B0_raw + 53;

And similarly for beta, with

  beta_raw ~ normal(0, 1);
  beta <- beta_raw * maxSlope;

- Bob

P.S. I find this kind of thing:

  B0 + beta *( x -2.)

very hard to read compared to the more standard

  B0 + beta * (x - 2)

or even "2.0" in place of "2" (you only need to do that if
all the expressions are integers and you don't want the
rounded down integer result).

