Hi Bob,

I've tried the approach you suggested and this did not change the result. As I increase the scale of the errors on x, the slope becomes more biased towards shallower values.

As I check, I place my simulated data into the algorithm of Kelly, B. C. (2007). Some Aspects of Measurement Error in Linear Regression of Astronomical Data. The Astrophysical Journal, 665(2), 1489–1506. http://doi.org/10.1086/519947

This algorithm includes intrinsic scatter, but I do not simulate any and it correctly finds that I do not. It finds the correct slope.

After some searching, I found these slides: http://ecastrostat2013.sciencesconf.org/conference/ecastrostat2013/pages/astrostat13_andreon.pdf
And on slide 36, they seem to be tackling the same problem, though they include intrinsic scatter.

So it seems this is possible, I'm just not sure why it is not working. Thanks!

/J. Michael

