On Thursday, 19 November 2015 21:50:27 UTC, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> Yes, they should be.  Weren't they in the files you attached?
> Or is the problem that the versions in our repos are missing
> the bounds?  If so, we'll need a pull request to fix that or
> an issue report and someone else will get to it eventually.
>

Yes, they are:

parameters {
....
  real<lower=0> sigmasq_delta;
....
}
transformed parameters {
  real<lower=0> sigma_delta;
  sigma_delta <- sqrt(sigmasq_delta);
}

I tracked the exception down to the inverse gamma prior; replacing this with a uniform(0,10) on the sd scale deals with this (incidentally, the Openbugs example linked to from the blocker example page uses the inverse gamma; the version included with WinBUGS 1.4.3 has uniform(0,10) on the standard deviation scale).

I'm still getting divergent transitions on the (attached) model with the uniform(0,10) prior when I have delta  ~ normal(d, sigma_delta); (this is the case even with adapt_delta=0.99).  Looking at the output in ShinyStan, it looks like these are all occurring when sigma_delta is (very close to) 0. I'm not getting any divergent transitions with the t-distribution model, with the same uniform prior on sigma_delta.  I don't understand why this is.

David

> I'm going to be taking a pass over all the models soon after
> we complete a few enhancements to the language.
>
> - Bob
>
> > On Nov 19, 2015, at 4:26 PM, David Manheim <davidm...@gmail.com> wrote:
> >
> > Bob - Shouldn't standard deviations be constrained to be positive?
> >
> >
> > On Wednesday, November 18, 2015 at 1:49:27 PM UTC-8, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> > I don't see anything you're not constraining properly in
> > this model.
> >
> > We need to update all these Stan programs to bring them in line
> > with our current recommendations.  As is, they're fairly
> > literal translations of the BUGS models.  I'm postponing that
> > until all the new language upgrades are in so I don't have to do
> > it all twice.
> >
> > The messages from the divergent transitions will tell you
> > what function is overflowing or underflowing.  I'm still not
> > clear on how RStan is dealing with these now during I/O.
> >
> > - Bob
> >
> > > On Nov 18, 2015, at 4:37 PM, Andrew Gelman <gel...@stat.columbia.edu> wrote:
