I haven't found one. I've gone over several papers related to this presentation and done a lot of simulations now to try and sort out the issue. I still get a slight bias in the slope which one would expect when not accounting for measurement error.... but the bias grows when the error on x_obs gets large. I would think that it should be taken care of by the measurement error model!

Here is a simpler version for just a line with some measurement error that I've been playing with:

data{

    int<lower=0> N;           // Number of points

    // Actual data
    vector[N] x_obs;          // x
    vector[N] y_obs;          // y
    real x_sig;          // x error
    real y_sig;          // y error
    }

parameters {

    vector[N] x_true;      // unknown true x
    vector[N] y_true;      // y without scatter

    real<lower=0>int_scatter_sq; //Instrinsic scatter squared

     // Group mu parameters

     real alpha;
     real beta;

 }

transformed parameters {

    real<lower=0> int_scatter; //Instrinsic scatter
     int_scatter <- sqrt(int_scatter_sq);
 }

model {

    beta  ~ normal(0, 2);
    alpha  ~ normal(0, 5);

    int_scatter_sq ~ inv_gamma(1E-5,1E-5);

    // Set meausurement error priors

    x_true ~ uniform(-5,5);

    x_obs ~ normal(x_true,x_sig); // measurement model

    ///////////////////////////////////////////

    y_true ~ normal(alpha+ beta*x_true, int_scatter);

    y_obs ~ normal(y_true,y_sig);

}

So the simulations here are simple:

x_true ~ uniform(-5,5)
y_true ~ normal(1+x_true,int_scatter)
x_obs ~ normal(x_true,x_sig)
y_obs ~ normal(y_true,y_sig)

I'm assuming homosedastic errors for now... trying to keep it simple. The intrinsic scatter just blurs y a bit.

Still, I'm having this bias problem with beta.... I've pretty much translated this from JAGS and from standard texts. Am I just missing something completely in Stan's implementation?

